<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matteo Maria Boiardo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1441">1441</a>–<a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matteo Maria Boiardo was an Italian poet and courtier at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> under Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este">Ercole I d'Este</a>. His unfinished chivalric epic Orlando Innamorato, published from the 1480s, combined the Carolingian legends with Arthurian romance and inspired Ariosto's Orlando Furioso. He also served the Este as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and of Reggio.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matteo-maria-boiardo.jpg" alt="Matteo Maria Boiardo" width="240" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matteo_Maria_Boiardo.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
